A||||14||Coop88||Replacing 88 front hubs||||1||
Z||000000||Coop88||10-26-2001||03:22 PM||||How long should it take to replace the front hubs on an 88? Should I have a torch handy? Mine died and I have to change them very soon. The car probably won't make it to Indy (one hour away), which is where I usually work on it. Looks like this is gonna be at a friend's house or in the parking lot. Why do things always break in cold weather?||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||10-26-2001||03:29 PM||||I hate to tell you but you probably won't find new hubs for the 88.  They no longer make them and nobody has found a supplier for them.  You will need to find a set of used 88 hubs.  Good luck. <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||10-26-2001||05:14 PM||||Ski,<P>He bought some good used ones from me, which are much better than the alternative!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Swapping them out isn't all that difficult.  First, jack up the car and remove the front wheels.  Now remove the calipers from the spindle (no need to remove the hydraulic line) and remove the rotor and the metal shield (held on by 3-10mm screws).  This is a great time for a brake job if it needs it.<P>Once this stuff is out of the way you can access the wheel bearings/hubs.  They are bolted to the spindle by 3 T-55 torx bolts and they are on there tight.  I highly recommend using an impact wrench on these as they are buggers to get off.  You can soak them with penetrating oil and/or heat them up if you want.....it certainly won't hurt anything.  Once the torx bolts are off you can remove the hub assembly.  This may take some time and some persuasion, but they will come out.  <P>Now install the new (new to you anyways) hubs and torque everything down.....you're done.<P>E-mail me if you need any help in doing this.<P>BTW.....I got your check today and will ship out those hubs tomorrow (you should get them by Tuesday or Wed.).   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||10-26-2001||07:05 PM||||I don't know if it is still the case, but I a month ago, my local AutoZone was able to order front hubs for '88. $99/each. I haven't ordered them yet(that was when my bad check fiasco was beginning so I held off), but plan to shortly. Again, if they are still available through them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||10-26-2001||08:11 PM||||Drew,<P>The hubs are no longer made but Timkin still makes the bearings. The bearings are pressed in on the '88. You can get the bearings and have a machine shop press the old ones out and press the new ones in.........<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fred88GT||10-26-2001||08:25 PM||||TGOWENS,<P>Do you know the part number for the bearing<BR>without the hub?<P>Fred||||reg||1
Z||000006||FTF Engineering||10-26-2001||09:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>The hubs are no longer made but Timkin still makes the bearings. The bearings are pressed in on the '88. You can get the bearings and have a machine shop press the old ones out and press the new ones in.........<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The '88 front hubs are <B>not</B> simply pressed together. They are held together with a forcibly crimped non-reusable collar on the inboard end of the spindle shaft.<P>They are not "serviceable" unless you cut the collar off and replace with a new one (and have the means to swage the new one in place on the shaft).<P>Unless your machine has the collars and the machine to crimp it into place, you are out of luck.<P>AND... If that isn't enough, there is no outer bearing race. The balls run directly on the hub housing itself. The implication is if a ball goes bad, the hub housing will probably be galled beyond usability anyway.<P>Are you sure you weren't thinking of the pre-88 front bearings?<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||10-26-2001||11:31 PM||||Fred88GT, we have talked to Timkin but I won't be able to find the part number until I get in the shop on Monday.<P>Bruce, at Fiero Shop that is all that we work on is Fieros and I see the '88s all the time. Yes we do have a press here and yes we have '88 hubs as well. So, no, I was <I>not</I> thinking of the pre-'88s. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rodmcneill||10-27-2001||01:28 AM||||Bruce at FTF, thanks for the explaination of how the 88 hub goes together, I also thought the old bearing could be pressed out and a new one pressed in, I did not realise what was involved. I contacted a local bearing supply house a couple of months ago and inquired about the Timken bearing.The parts man called Timken direct and they informed him they would not sell the bearing separate, now I know why, probably a liability concern.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FTF Engineering||10-28-2001||07:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rodmcneill:<BR><B>Bruce at FTF, thanks for the explaination of how the 88 hub goes together</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rod, Sure thing. Sorry I didn't have any easy answers for you though.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>I see the '88s all the time. Yes we do have a press here and yes we have '88 hubs as well. So, no, I was not thinking of the pre-'88s.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>tgowens, The <B>REAR</B> hubs are pressed together on all years... Maybe you were thinking of the <B>REAR</B> hubs???<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
